1. If browsers follow the same-origin policy, how can clickjacking still work?
Many people think that because of Same-Origin Policy, attackers cannot interact with another site. 
But clickjacking does not violate the same-origin policy at all.
The key insight is this:
Clickjacking does not read or modify the target page. It only tricks the user into clicking it.
Let’s see how.

Imagine a banking website:
https://bank.com/transfer
This page contains a button:
Transfer $1000
Now the attacker creates a malicious website:
https://evil.com/freegift
Inside this page, the attacker embeds the bank page in an iframe.
<iframe src="https://bank.com/transfer"></iframe>

The attacker then makes the iframe transparent and places it under a fake button.
Visual layout:
---------------------------------
|  Click here to win an iPhone  |
|                               |
|   (actually bank transfer)    |
---------------------------------

CSS makes the bank page invisible:
opacity: 0
position: absolute

The user thinks they are clicking:
Win iPhone
But their click lands on the bank transfer button inside the iframe.
The browser allows this because:
• the page is not reading bank.com data
• it is only embedding the page
So Same-Origin Policy is not violated.

2. Why is clickjacking especially dangerous for state-changing requests
Clickjacking only becomes impactful when a click performs a sensitive action.
Let’s imagine a cloud console:
https://cloud.example.com/deleteVM
The page contains:
[ Delete Production Server ]
If an attacker can frame this page and trick the user into clicking it, the action happens immediately.

The attack works because most web applications:
• keep users logged in via cookies
• automatically include authentication in requests

So when the user clicks the button inside the iframe, the browser sends:
Cookie: session=abc123
The request becomes authenticated.

Example request:
POST /deleteVM
Cookie: session=abc123

The server thinks:
User intentionally clicked delete
But the user actually clicked something else.
This is why clickjacking is particularly dangerous for:
• money transfers
• account changes
• admin actions
• permission grants

3. Why is X-Frame-Options not enough protection today?
Older defenses relied on the header:
X-Frame-Options
Example:
X-Frame-Options: DENY
or
X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN

This tells the browser:
Do not allow this page to be loaded inside an iframe
However, modern applications have complex embedding requirements.
For example:
app.company.com
dashboard.company.com
support.company.com
These domains may need to embed each other.

X-Frame-Options is limited because it only supports:
DENY
SAMEORIGIN
It cannot specify:
Allow these specific domains

That is why modern systems use **Content Security Policy with the frame-ancestors directive.
Example:
Content-Security-Policy:
frame-ancestors 'self' https://dashboard.company.com

This allows fine-grained control over who can frame the page.

4. Why do some clickjacking attacks require multiple clicks?
Many developers think:
A single click attack is easy to notice
So modern attacks use multi-step UI manipulation.
Example scenario:
A cloud platform has:
Step 1: Create API key
Step 2: Confirm permission
The attacker overlays two transparent iframes.
Visual layering:
Layer 1: Fake game button
Layer 2: API key creation page
Layer 3: confirmation page

The victim thinks they are playing a game:
Click to shoot aliens
Click again to reload
But each click triggers a real action in the iframe.

Click 1 → Create API key
Click 2 → Approve admin scope

Now the attacker has an API key with privileges.
This is why clickjacking can become a full account takeover vector.

5. Why does CSRF protection not always stop clickjacking?
This is one of the most confusing questions.
People often assume:
CSRF token = protected from clickjacking
But CSRF tokens only protect against forged requests.
Clickjacking sends legitimate user clicks.
Example:
A page contains:
<form action="/transfer">
<input type="hidden" name="csrf" value="abc123">
<button>Transfer</button>

The CSRF token is already embedded in the page.
When the user clicks the button inside the iframe, the browser submits:
POST /transfer
csrf=abc123

The server sees:
Valid CSRF token
Valid session
So the request succeeds.

This shows the difference:
CSRF = forged request
Clickjacking = real user click
That is why CSRF protection does not solve clickjacking.

-> How modern systems mitigate clickjacking:
Production systems usually combine multiple defenses.
• First, they prevent framing entirely.
  Content-Security-Policy:
  frame-ancestors 'none'
  This blocks embedding.
• Second, they restrict allowed parent sites.
  frame-ancestors 'self'
• Third, they add user interaction verification for sensitive actions.
  Example:
  Re-enter password
  Confirm dialog
  2FA step
• Fourth, they avoid dangerous UI patterns like single-click destructive actions.

-> The mental model that makes clickjacking easy to remember:
Think of clickjacking as a visual deception attack.
The browser executes exactly what the user clicked.
The attacker simply changes what the user thinks they clicked.
User intention  ≠  Actual click target
That mismatch is the vulnerability.


Real World Use case:

Clickjacking is formally called UI Redressing, and it often interacts with protections like Content Security Policy and X-Frame-Options.
The goal is to understand where it appears, why it appears, and how attackers chain it into real compromises.

Attack Chain 1 — OAuth Permission Grant Clickjacking:
This is one of the most realistic attacks against SaaS platforms.
System Architecture:
User Browser
     │
     ▼
Attacker Website (evil.com)
     │
     │ iframe
     ▼
Identity Provider OAuth Consent Screen
     │
     ▼
User grants permissions to attacker app

The Setup:
A SaaS platform uses OAuth 2.0.
When users log in with third-party apps, they see a permission screen like:
Application: PhotoEditor Pro
Requests permission:
✔ Read your files
✔ Upload files
✔ Delete files

Normally, the user must click “Allow.”

The Clickjacking Attack:
The attacker creates a malicious page:
evil.com/freephoto
This page loads the OAuth consent page in an invisible iframe.
<iframe src="https://auth.company.com/oauth/consent">

The attacker overlays a fake button:
CLICK HERE TO DOWNLOAD FREE IMAGE
But the click lands on:
[Allow]
on the OAuth screen.

Result:
The attacker’s application now has OAuth access tokens to the victim's account.
Example API access:
GET /files
POST /upload
DELETE /file

-> Why this works:-
Developers often protect login pages but forget to protect consent pages.

-> Mitigation:-
Consent pages must include:
Content-Security-Policy:
frame-ancestors 'none'
and require explicit confirmation steps.

Attack Chain 2 — Cloud Console Destructive Action:
This scenario happens in internal admin consoles.
Architecture:
Engineer Browser
      │
      ▼
Attacker Page
      │
      ▼
Framed Cloud Admin Console
      │
      ▼
Delete Production Resource

-> Example Target:
A cloud dashboard contains:
Delete Database
Delete VM
Revoke Access

If the console allows framing, the attacker embeds it:
<iframe src="https://cloud.company.com/deleteVM">
Then the attacker overlays a fake game.
Click here to shoot enemies
When the user clicks repeatedly:
Click #1 → selects VM
Click #2 → confirm delete

-> Result:
Production infrastructure is destroyed.
This is why cloud consoles must block framing.

-> Mitigation:
Admin consoles should always set:
X-Frame-Options: DENY
or CSP:
frame-ancestors 'none'

Attack Chain 3 — Clickjacking + CSRF Token Reuse:
Developers often assume **Cross-Site Request Forgery protections stop UI attacks.
But they do not.

-> Architecture:
User logged into bank
       │
       ▼
Attacker page
       │
       ▼
Framed bank transfer page
       │
       ▼
User clicks hidden transfer button

-> Why CSRF protection fails:-
A bank page might contain:
<input type="hidden" name="csrf_token" value="ABC123">
The token is already inside the page.
When the user clicks the hidden button:
POST /transfer
csrf_token=ABC123
Cookie=session123

The server sees:
Valid token
Valid session
So the request succeeds.

-> Result:
The attacker tricks the user into approving a transaction.

-> Mitigation:
For sensitive operations:
• require confirmation dialogs
• require re-authentication
• disable framing

Attack Chain 4 — Clickjacking + Drag-and-Drop File Upload:
Modern browsers support drag-and-drop UI events.
Attackers can combine these with clickjacking.

Architecture:
User Browser
     │
     ▼
Attacker Site
     │
     ▼
Framed File Upload Page

-> Attack Flow:
The attacker creates a fake game.

Drag the image into the box to start

But the drop target overlaps a hidden iframe:
https://cloud.company.com/upload

The browser interprets the drop event as:
File upload

The victim unknowingly uploads a sensitive file.

-> Real Impact:
This can expose:
• SSH keys
• configuration files
• private images

-> Mitigation:
Upload pages should:
• disable framing
• validate user interaction
• require explicit file selection

Attack Chain 5 — Clickjacking + Multi-Step Privilege Escalation:
This is the most sophisticated UI attack.
Attackers manipulate multiple clicks.
Architecture:

Victim Browser
      │
      ▼
Attacker Website
      │
      ▼
Framed Admin Settings

-> Target Action:
Admin page contains:
Grant Admin Role
But it requires two steps:
Step 1: Select user
Step 2: Confirm

-> The Attack:
The attacker overlays multiple transparent frames.
Visual stack:
Layer 1: fake button
Layer 2: user selection page
Layer 3: confirmation dialog

The victim plays a fake game:
Click to start
Click again to continue

Each click triggers:
Click #1 → select attacker account
Click #2 → confirm admin role

-> Result:
The attacker becomes an administrator.

-> Why clickjacking still appears in modern systems:-
Even mature companies sometimes miss these protections because:
• microservices expose internal dashboards
• OAuth consent pages are overlooked
• developers assume CSRF tokens are enough
• embedding is needed for widgets and integrations

-> The architecture-level mental model:-
The easiest way to understand clickjacking in system design is:
User perception layer
        │
        ▼
Browser rendering layer
        │
        ▼
Application logic

Clickjacking manipulates the perception layer.
The browser executes the click exactly as rendered.

-> Interview-ready takeaway:
A strong answer usually ends like this:
Clickjacking is not just an iframe issue. It is a UI trust problem where attackers manipulate visual layers so the user performs unintended actions with their authenticated session. 
The most dangerous cases appear when clickjacking combines with OAuth permission screens, cloud admin consoles, and multi-step workflows.
